governor turn 1: ask  ("Easier to read" could mean several different output changes, and each one would need a different test. Right now `todo list` prints lines like "[ ] buy milk" with no ids or due dates. I need to know which changes the user wants before I can write a contract.)
governor asks: What should "easier to read" mean for the `todo list` output? Right now it prints lines like "[ ] buy milk" / "[x] walk dog", with no ids and no due dates. Please pick any of these, or describe your own:
(a) Show each todo's id, e.g. "#1 [ ] buy milk", so you know what to pass to `todo complete`.
(b) Show the due date when there is one, e.g. "#1 [ ] buy milk (due 2026-11-01)".
(c) Line up the columns (id, status, title, due) so they align across rows.
(d) Sort open todos first, or by due date.
(e) Add a summary line, e.g. "2 open, 1 done".
(f) Use colour in the terminal (e.g. dim the finished ones).
Should the messages from add, complete and errors also change, or only the list?

